<cell><m>2</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
<cell><m>10</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
Vertical intercept is <m>y=5</m>, and there are no horizontal intercepts (<m>y</m> is never <m>0</m>).
</p>
</solution>
</task>



<task workspace="1in">
<statement>

<tabular left="minor" right="minor" top="minor" bottom="minor" halign="center">
<row bottom="minor">
<cell><m>x</m></cell>
<cell><m>3</m></cell>
<cell><m>4</m></cell>
<cell><m>5</m></cell>
<cell><m>6</m></cell>
<cell><m>7</m></cell>
<cell><m>8</m></cell>
</row>
<row>
<cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
<cell><m>-5</m></cell>
<cell><m>-4</m></cell>
<cell><m>-5</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>-2</m></cell>
<cell><m>0</m></cell>
</row>
</tabular>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
There are no vertical intercepts (<m>x</m> is never <m>0</m>), and the horizontal intercept is at <m>x=8</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>



</exercise>





<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
Estimate the horizontal and vertical intercepts given the graph of the function.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_1_4_1">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_1_4_1_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=0,xmax=8,
ymin=-5,ymax=5,
xtick={1,...,8},
ytick={-4,...,4},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
trig format = rad,
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=0:8,thick] {4*sin(2*pi/8*x)};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The vertical intercept is at <m>(0,0)</m>. The horizontal intercepts are at <m>(0,0)</m>, <m>(4,0)</m>, and <m>(8,0)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
Estimate the horizontal and vertical intercepts given the graph of the function.
</p>

<image width="50%" xml:id="ws_1_4_2">
<shortdescription>
A short description.
</shortdescription>
<latex-image label="ws_1_4_2_fig">
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\begin{axis}[grid=both,
axis lines=middle,
xmin=-2,xmax=4,
ymin=-1,ymax=9,
xtick={-1,1,2,3},
ytick={1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8},
minor tick={},
xlabel=\(x\), ylabel=\(y\),
samples=250]
\addplot[domain=-2:4,thick] {(x-1)^2+2};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
</latex-image>
</image>

</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The vertical intercept is at <m>(0,3)</m>. There are no horizontal intercepts.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise workspace="1in">
<statement>
<p>
A ball is dropped from a cliff above a lake. The ball's height above the surface of the lake, <m>h(t)</m>, in feet, <m>t</m> seconds after the ball is dropped is given by
<md>
h(t)=300-16t^2.
</md>
Find the vertical intercept of the function <m>h(t)</m> and the horizontal intercepts for which <m>t\geq 0</m>. (Round off to two decimal places.) Interpret the intercepts in practical terms.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The vertical intercept is <m>y=h(0)=300</m>. This occurs when <m>t=0</m>, so we can say that the ball was <m>300</m> feet above ground when it was dropped.

The horizontal intercept is when <m>y=h(t)=0</m>, that is when <m>0=300-16t^2</m>. Solving for <m>t</m> we get
<md>
16t^2 = 300 \quad \implies \quad t^2 = \frac{300}{16} \quad \implies \quad t = \sqrt{300 / 16} \approx 4.33.
</md> This means that <m>4.33</m> seconds after the ball was dropped, it hit the ground (because the height <m>h(t)</m> is <m>0</m>).
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>



<exercise>
<statement>
<p>
The value of a car <m>V(t)</m>, in dollars, <m>t</m> years after the car was purchased is:
<md>
V(t)=21500-1500t
</md>
Find vertical and horizontal intercepts of the function <m>V(t)</m> and interpret them in terms of dollars and years.
</p>
</statement>

<solution>
<p>
The vertical intercept is when <m>t=0</m>, which is <m>V(0)=21500</m>. This means that the car was worth <m>\$21500</m> at the time of purchase.

The horizontal intercept is when <m>V(t)=0</m>. That is, <m>21500-1500t=0</m>. Solving this equation for <m>t</m> yields
<md>
21500 = 1500t \quad \implies \quad t = \frac{21500}{1500}  \approx 14.33.
</md> Therefore, <m>14.33</m> years after purchase the value of the car is <m>\$0</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>





</worksheet>